\begin_layout Standard
We humans often tend to over-complicate things.
 In the past,
 I’ve talked about ‘
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Lies to Children"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/162237869/lies-to-children"
literal "false"

\end_inset

’,⌘ where we tell a convenient ‘lie’ in order to get a concept across,
 while the truth—
or a better model—
is more mysterious and difficult.
 The example I gave was the Bohr model of the hydrogen atom,
 which is pretty much wrong in every particular.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On reflection though,
 there’s a sort of polar opposite,
 where we have a damn good model that’s also simple,
 and we clothe this in mystery to the point where people are so intimidated they give up.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take rainbows.
 If we read “
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "The Theory of the Rainbow"
target "http://bionics.seas.ucla.edu/education/MAE_182A/Airy_Eq_Rainbows.pdf"
literal "false"

\end_inset

” by H.
 Moyses Nussenzveig in Scientific American from 1977,
 we might come away thinking that in order to ‘really’ understand rainbows,
 we need to embrace both the 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Airy function"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airy_function"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 (for a basic understanding of the intensity of scattered light within the rainbow) and fine computation of how electromagnetic plane waves are scattered by raindrops,
 involving “complex angular momentum” or the 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Sommerfeld-Watson transform"
target "https://scipedia.bohrium.com/en/sciencepedia/feynman/keyword/complex_angular_momentum"
literal "false"

\end_inset

!
 All of which might muddy the elegant,
 basic maths of the rainbow for the average reader,
 explained 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "rather well by Descartes in 1637"
target "https://www.ams.org/publicoutreach/feature-column/fcarc-rainbows"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

