\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The sunlight comes in from A,
 it is refracted in the spherical raindrop,
 internally reflects once off C,
 exits at D and is perceived by the observer at E.
 The geometry is such that the angle DEM is about 42 degrees,
 but because different wavelengths of light undergo different degrees of refraction (Newton’s prism experiments of 1666),
 the spectrum is displayed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Similar simple reasoning"
target "https://www.physicstheories.com/physics-of-rainbows"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 explains different rainbows seen with droplets that have a different refractive index,
 as well as a fainter secondary rainbow with reversed colours 10 degrees outside the primary one (two internal reflections),
 and the dark area between the primary and secondary rainbows.
 And yes,
 of course it’s more complex,
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
There are third,
 fourth and fifth order rainbows—
and beyond.
 Fake-looking triple rainbows arise due to reflection of sunlight off a body of water.
 Supernumerary bands (‘stacker rainbows’) can arise from optical interference.
 And then there’s the polarisation,
 and all that complex maths.
 (Don’t mention glories,
 which aren’t rainbows).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 but we can still wonder and understand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly,
 we might expect that the internal workings of an entire computer language would be deep,
 dark,
 arcane and inscrutable—
and so on with many other complex-seeming things,
 both natural and created.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It ain’t necessarily so!
 I’d even go so far as to say that if something looks utterly inscrutable,
 we often just need to explain better—
or it’s built wrong,
 despite appearing to work.
 Let’s try this idea with Zyzyxia.
 Soon,
 we’ll write the core of the entire language in just seven lines.
 Promise.
 First,
 we need a little spadework.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
What we want
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We’ve already met the basic idea:
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "POWER"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/213376055/power"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.⌘ Write 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Polish notation"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/p/lets-polish-our-polish"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,⌘ throw away the parentheses,
 and parse from right-to-left.
 This gives us 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "the simplicity of J"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/212130688/j-has-parts-of-speechhttps://drjo.substack.com/i/212130688/j-has-parts-of-speech"
literal "false"

\end_inset

⌘ and 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Forth"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/213376055/going-forth-using-reverse-polish"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,⌘ with the elegance of 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Lisp"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/213376055/lisp"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.⌘
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The question is:
 “How do we implement this,
 simply?”.
 We can limit things a bit by deciding upfront that our language will be Web-based.
 This in turn invites a number of secondary questions,
 the most pressing being “Which language should I use to write it?”
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some languages have even been written mostly in themselves.
 For example,
 in about 1961,
 Timothy Hart and Michael Levin 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "wrote"
target "https://texdraft.github.io/lisp-compiler/internals.html"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 the first successful Lisp compiler—
in Lisp.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
An elegant,
 neatly-structured version is available if you’re interested.
 It reads quite well.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This was called ‘bootstrapping’,
 from the apparently impossible feat of pulling yourself up by your own bootstraps.
 It’s not that mysterious though:
 first write a Lisp interpreter,
 then use this to interpret the Lisp code,
 and then use Lisp to write your compiler.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Which for the non-computer people in the room,
 invites further questions:
 “What’s an interpreter?”,
 “What’s a compiler?”,
 and “Which one should we build?”.
\end_layout

