\begin_layout Section*
Compilers,
 then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A salacious comparison between interpreted and compiled code is that it’s the difference between watching sex and having sex.
 If a program is interpreted,
 then every instruction needs to be read in,
 the interpreter needs to say “What do I do next?”—
and then the interpreter painstakingly does what is required.
 A compiler translates the code into the native language used by the processor,
 and then just does it.
 Perhaps an even more clumsy analogy is that it’s the difference between (a) having sex and (b) painstakingly plodding through a sex manual,
 sentence-by-sentence,
 saying “What’s the next step then?” Um.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In further apparent support of compilation,
 it’s not unusual for compiled code to run one or even two orders of magnitude faster than interpreted instructions.
 Compilation however has its down-side.
 Optimised compilation can take ages.
 Actually writing the compiler for a given language is often non-trivial.
 And the code that is produced tends to be inscrutable,
 so debugging can be,
 well,
 interesting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interpretation has more subtle advantages.
 If your instructions are terse and the interpreter is small,
 you may even be able to fit the lot into a cache so that it just whirls around inside the processor.
 Modern processors are grunty.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We also find that in a given program,
 most of the running is done in just a tiny portion of the code—
so efficiency often comes from getting that code right.
 As we saw with 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "our Fibonacci code"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/220506032/aww-lets-play-with-phyllotaxis"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,⌘ it’s often more important to get the algorithm right than to worry about “compilation vs interpretation”.
 Done right,
 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{40,000}$
\end_inset

 was far faster than a naive 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{40}$
\end_inset

.
 There’s one other consideration that looms even larger.
\end_layout

