\begin_layout Quotation
Reporter:
 “Why did you want to climb Everest?”
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
George Mallory “Because it’s there” —
 New York Times,
 18 March 1923 (
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Perhaps"
target "https://gripped.com/news/george-mallory-might-never-have-said-because-its-there-about-everest/"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 a misattribution).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a prevalent myth that if someone is smart,
 insightful,
 innovative—
and they work hard enough for long enough—
then generally,
 they will succeed.
 This is bolstered by two biases.
 The first is an error of logic:
 we see that people who succeed can often be shown to have been smart,
 insightful,
 innovative,
 and hard-working.
 Unfortunately,
 this doesn’t tell us anything about the people who were smart,
 insightful,
 innovative,
 worked their arses off—
and failed.
 Some people die on mountains,
 you see.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The other bias is that people who succeed tend to make damn sure that they rewrite history to show that they indeed were smart,
 insightful,
 innovative and hard-working,
 even if the factors that actually drove their success were (a) luck,
 (b) being in the right place at the right time,
 (c) knowing the right people and (d) having wealthy parents.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Or stomping on the fingers of the opposition.
 Their ‘smarts’ may be in non-traditional or even not-that-intelligent domains.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When it comes to computer languages,
 things are even more fraught.
 Right at the beginning,
 it often seems to be the case that someone threw something together,
 and it took off for reasons no better than there was no other less shitty language at the time (NOLSLATT).
 Some languages arose out of personal projects infused with a bit of passion,
 Python being the archetypal example.
 Some like Smalltalk grew from kids’ education;
 others like Pascal arose from adult didactics.
 Perhaps C just met the need.
 C++ was commissioned.
 And others are just a mess.
 Take JavaScript.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But we will build Zyzyxia in JavaScript!
 It may be shitty,
 but it’s there.
 A little more justification may be required,
 though.
\end_layout

