\begin_layout Section*
A potted history of JavaScript
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A few years after I got onto the Internet,
 I remember experimenting with the first web browser that really took off:
 Mosaic.
 Boy was it crappy.
 In 1994 it was supplanted by Netscape Navigator.
 This was still rather crap.
 One of the biggest problems was that pages were 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "static"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Static_web_page"
literal "false"

\end_inset

—
so in 1995,
 Netscape decided to add a programming language.
 As Java was the flavour of the day,
 this was the initial target,
 with some elements from Lisp (Scheme,
 to be precise).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It was flung together,
 and succeeded entirely due to NOLSLATT.
 Microsoft soon muscled in (“
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "embrace, extend, extinguish"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embrace,_extend,_and_extinguish"
literal "false"

\end_inset

” anyone?) resulting in hugely variable behaviour across browsers.
 Later,
 things settled down into a NOLSLATT standard.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then in the mid-2000’s,
 Mozilla Firefox shook things up,
 and stole market from Microsoft.
 A 2005 white paper spurred the development of a technique dubbed ‘Ajax’ that allowed the browser to load data ‘asynchronously’ in the background,
 and in 2008 Google produced a JavaScript engine that compiled the code,
 blitzing the competition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
JavaScript had finally come of age,
 and to this day,
 the Web pretty much runs on it.
 Which in some ways is a pity,
 because it’s actually still mostly a bulky,
 shitty language,
 built around an even more bulky and crapulous document object model,
 or DOM.
 Note that ‘
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "crapulous"
target "https://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/crapulous"
literal "false"

\end_inset

’ doesn’t mean crap—
instead it means that it suffers from the consequences of excessive eating or alcohol consumption,
 adequately describing the flatulently bloated DOM,
 and supporting the conjecture that it was largely a by-product of beer and bad pizza.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So,
 let’s build a language in JavaScript!
 But if it’s so shitty,
 why use it?
 One answer is the mountaineer’s one:
 “Because it’s there”.
 Another answer is NOLSLATT.
 A third extends this second answer—
TypeScript has some features that make it attractive to some developers,
 but (a) is hugely dependent on one company,
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Microsoft.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 always a danger sign;
 (b) obscures some things and obfuscates others;
 and (c) is ultimately deviously translated to JavaScript,
 anyway.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another ‘option’,
 WebAssembly (
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "WASM"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WebAssembly"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) is currently a bit obscure and constrained,
 and can be cumbersome.
 And unless you want to write your own browser and language and enter into an arse-bashing competition with the ultimate porcupine,
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Google.
 Practically,
 Chrome has a monopoly.
 This is why I use Mozilla Firefox.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 those are pretty much your choices.
 So \SpecialChar ldots

\end_layout

